# {{ project_name }}

A data product on Databricks. [dlt](https://dlthub.com) lands the data{% if include_dbt %}, [dbt](https://getdbt.com)
shapes it{% elif include_stevin %}, a SQL task
shapes it{% endif %}, one job runs {% if include_dbt or include_stevin %}both{% else %}it{% endif %}, and the schemas are the bundle's. Made with
[data-product-template](https://github.com/kostavo-oss/data-product-template), run with
[leeghwater](https://github.com/kostavo-oss/tools/tree/main/packages/leeghwater){% if include_stevin %}, its tables and access declared with
[stevin](https://kostavo-oss.github.io/tools/stevin/){% endif %}{% if include_lely %}, deployed with
[lely](https://github.com/kostavo-oss/tools/tree/main/packages/lely){% endif %}{% if include_caland %}, its secrets kept with
[caland](https://github.com/kostavo-oss/tools/tree/main/packages/caland){% endif %}.

```
src/{{ package_name }}/pipelines/   the dlt pipelines: one function each, marked @pipeline
{%- if include_dbt %}
dbt/models/                 the dbt models, reading raw through source('raw', ...)
{%- elif include_stevin %}
sql/                        the SQL the job runs, with no schema name in it
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_stevin %}
stevin/                     the tables {% if include_dbt %}dbt does not own{% else %}and who may read them{% endif %}, as specs
{%- endif %}
resources/schemas.yml       raw{% if include_dbt or include_stevin %}, raw_staging, silver{% else %} and raw_staging{% endif %} — the bundle makes them, the job gets their names
resources/*.job.yml         the job: ingest{% if include_dbt or include_stevin %} → transform{% endif %}
{%- if include_contracts %}
dataproduct.yaml            the product and its ports
contracts/                  what each port promises, one file per version
{%- endif %}
mise.toml                   every task; `mise tasks` lists them
```

## Here, with nothing but Python

```sh
mise install            # python, uv, the Databricks CLI{% if include_lely %}, lely{% endif %}{% if include_stevin %}, stevin{% endif %}{% if include_caland %}, caland{% endif %}
uv sync
mise run dev            # the example pipeline, into example.duckdb
mise run check          # lint, format, the pipelines import{% if include_dbt %}, dbt parses{% endif %}{% if include_contracts %}, the contracts are valid{% endif %}{% if include_stevin %}, the table specs are valid{% endif %}
```

`uv run ingest list` names the pipelines; `uv run ingest run example --count 3` runs one
with an option. dlt keeps a pipeline's state under `~/.dlt/pipelines/<name>/`, so to start
over, delete that folder and the `.duckdb` file.

## On your workspace: the dev target

Copy `mise.local.toml.example` to `mise.local.toml` and fill in your profile from
`~/.databrickscfg`, your warehouse and the catalog. Then:

```sh
mise run deploy         # the schemas, the job and the wheel, prefixed with your name
{%- if include_stevin and not include_dbt %}
mise run tables:apply   # the table the job fills: stevin plans, shows, asks, makes it
{%- endif %}
mise run job            # the job, on the workspace
{%- if include_stevin and include_dbt %}
mise run tables:apply   # tags and grants on the table the job landed, after a look
{%- endif %}
mise run ingest -- example    # a pipeline from here, into the dev target's schemas
{%- if include_dbt %}
mise run transform      # dbt from here, against the same schemas, with the CLI's sign-in
{%- endif %}
mise run doctor         # what a run from here would use, checked
```

In development mode every schema and the job carry a prefix with your name, so two people
on one workspace never meet. The job is told the deployed names: nothing in the code knows
them.

{%- if include_lely %}

## Deploying for real: lely

`mise run plan` shows what a deploy would change and changes nothing; `mise run apply`
does it after you have looked; `mise run destroy` takes the target down again. On GitHub
the same happens on every pull request (`.github/workflows/plan.yml` posts the plan as a
comment) and on merge (`apply.yml` applies the plan that was reviewed, not a new one). The
repository needs `DATABRICKS_HOST` and `WAREHOUSE_ID` as variables and `DATABRICKS_TOKEN`
as a secret. The first push to `main` shows a failed `apply`, because `apply` only applies
a plan that was reviewed on a pull request; the first pull request puts that right.

`databricks bundle deploy -t prod` works without lely; lely adds the plan and the review.
{%- if include_stevin and not include_dbt %}

The tables are a step of the same deploy: `tables` in `lely.yml`, after `bundle`, runs
`stevin apply` (`ops/tables.py`). lely's plan shows that step as one run and not what it
would change; `mise run tables:plan` shows that.
{%- elif include_stevin %}

stevin is no step of this deploy. Its one spec governs a table the job lands, and that
table is not there when a new target is first deployed. `mise run tables:apply` runs it,
after the job has run once.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_stevin %}

## Tables and access: stevin

[stevin](https://kostavo-oss.github.io/tools/stevin/) keeps the tables and the access
{% if include_dbt %}that dbt does not own{% else %}of this product{% endif %}. A spec in `stevin/tables/` says what a table should be; stevin
compares that with the workspace, shows the difference as a plan, and applies it.

```sh
mise run tables:validate    # the specs, checked here; also part of `mise run check`
mise run tables:plan        # what would change on the dev target; changes nothing
mise run tables:apply       # the plan, shown, and run after a yes
mise run tables:drift       # does the target still match the specs
```

stevin runs at a deploy and never in the job. The job only reads and writes rows.
{%- if include_dbt %}

**What is stevin's here.** dbt builds `squares` and owns it: its grants and tags go in
dbt's config, and stevin refuses a spec for it. dlt lands `numbers` and owns its shape.
`stevin/tables/numbers.yml` names no column types, so it only governs that table: a tag
and a grant. `stevin/stevin.yml` says whose table is whose, and a new dbt model gets a
line there. The spec's group is a placeholder: replace it with a group of the account
before the first apply. The first apply comes after the first run of the job, because
stevin does not make a table it does not shape.
{%- else %}

**Where a table's shape lives.** `stevin/tables/squares.yml` is the table `squares`.
{%- if include_contracts %}
Its columns, types, key and descriptions come from the contract,
`contracts/output/squares/v1.odcs.yaml`: a change to the shape is a change there, and
the spec adds the clustering, the tags and the grants.
{%- else %}
Its columns, types and key are in that file, with the clustering, the tags and the
grants.
{%- endif %}
`sql/squares.sql` fills the table in the job's `transform` task and never makes it. The
first apply comes after the first deploy and before the first run of the job.

**Add a table.** A spec in `stevin/tables/`, a `.sql` file in `sql/` that writes its
rows, and a task for the file in `resources/{{ project_name }}.job.yml`. The file takes the
catalog and the schemas as parameters and names none.
{%- endif %}

**What is not stevin's.** The schemas are the bundle's (`resources/schemas.yml`), with
their grants: a spec names one as `${resources.schemas.<key>.name}` and stevin makes
none. The targets and the variables are the bundle's too. Groups come from the identity
provider, and stevin cannot make one. Policies that mask by tag are written in Terraform
or SQL.

stevin is pinned in `mise.toml`, under `[tools]`: every release so far is a pre-release.
{%- if include_access_examples %}

**An access model to start from** is in `stevin/security/`: groups per schema, rows by
region, PII by tag. Its specs are checked by `mise run check` and are not planned until
`stevin/stevin.yml` lists the folder. `stevin/security/README.md` says what to replace
first.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}

## Secrets

A source asks dlt for a secret: `api_key: str = dlt.secrets.value`. dlt looks in the
environment, then in `.dlt/secrets.toml` on your laptop, then in the secret scope the app
names, under dlt's path joined with `-`:

```sh
databricks secrets create-scope {% if secret_scope %}{{ secret_scope }}{% else %}<scope>{% endif %}
databricks secrets put-secret {% if secret_scope %}{{ secret_scope }}{% else %}<scope>{% endif %} sources-example-api_key
```
{%- if include_caland %}

`mise run secrets` opens caland on this bundle's workspace: the scopes, their secrets and
who may read them, in a page.
{%- endif %}
{%- if secret_scope %}

**The scope is a step.** `ops/scope.py` makes `{{ secret_scope }}` — before the bundle under
lely (`lely.yml`, step `scope`; `${steps.scope.name}` reaches the job as `${var.secret_scope}`),
or on its own, with no lely config:

```sh
uv run ops/scope.py plan -t dev --name {{ secret_scope }}      # shows what it would make
mise run scope                                           # makes it on your workspace
uv run ops/scope.py check -t dev --name {{ secret_scope }} --live
```

It is a lely step with its own command line; `uv run ops/scope.py --help` lists them.
{%- endif %}
{%- if not secret_scope %}

The app names no scope yet: give one in `src/{{ package_name }}/cli.py`
(`App(..., secret_scopes=["<scope>"])`) and pass it in the job (`--secret-scope`).
{%- endif %}

## Add a pipeline

A function marked `@pipeline` in `src/{{ package_name }}/pipelines/`; its parameters become
options of `ingest run <name>`. It makes its dlt pipeline with
`leeghwater.create_pipeline(...)` and runs it with `leeghwater.run(...)` — never with
`dlt.pipeline()`: where a run loads is the run's to say. Add a task for it in
`resources/{{ project_name }}.job.yml` with the options the example task passes.
{%- if include_dbt %}

## Add a model

A `.sql` file in `dbt/models/`, reading raw through `source('raw', '<table>')` (add the
table to `dbt/models/sources.yml`). `mise run check` parses it, `mise run transform` builds
it against the dev target, and the job's `transform` task builds it after `ingest`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if include_contracts %}

## Contracts

`dataproduct.yaml` lists the product's ports, and every port names a contract: a file that
says which columns a table has, of which type, and which are never empty. The output port
`{% if include_dbt or include_stevin %}squares{% else %}numbers{% endif %}` has `contracts/output/{% if include_dbt or include_stevin %}squares{% else %}numbers{% endif %}/v1.odcs.yaml`. It is a promise to whoever reads the
table, so it changes in the same pull request as the {% if include_dbt %}model{% elif include_stevin %}SQL{% else %}pipeline{% endif %}, and a change that breaks a
reader is a new file, `v2.odcs.yaml`, with its own entry in `dataproduct.yaml`. CI
compares the contract with the base branch and refuses a breaking edit.

```sh
mise run contracts:lint       # every contract is valid; also part of `mise run check`
{%- if not (include_dbt or include_stevin) %}
mise run contracts:test       # the table `mise run dev` loaded, against its contract
{%- endif %}
mise run contracts:catalog    # the contracts as pages, in site/contracts/
mise run contracts:edit       # the contract in an editor: a page served from this machine
{%- if include_dbt %}
uv run ops/ports.py dbt --dry-run   # what the contract would write into dbt/models/
mise run contracts:dbt        # write it: columns, descriptions and tests
{%- endif %}
```
{%- if include_dbt %}

The contract names one server, the deployed table: a dbt model is built on the workspace
only, so there is no local copy to test and no `contracts:test` task.
{%- elif include_stevin %}

The contract names one server, the deployed table: `squares` is filled on the workspace
only, so there is no local copy to test and no `contracts:test` task. stevin makes the
table from the same file, so its shape is written once.
{%- endif %}

To read another product's table, add an input port: `contracts/input/README.md` says how.
`mise run contracts:pull` keeps the producer's contract as a snapshot, and
`mise run contracts:check` says whether the producer still keeps it.

`mise run contracts:dlt` writes a dlt schema from every snapshot into
`src/{{ package_name }}/schemas/`, with every table frozen. A pipeline that loads the port is
named after it and names the folder, and its run then fails on a row with a column the
contract does not have:

```python
SCHEMAS = Path(__file__).parent.parent / "schemas"

p = leeghwater.create_pipeline("<port>", import_schema_path=SCHEMAS)
```

`ops/ports.py` does this by running the [Data Contract CLI](https://github.com/datacontract/datacontract-cli)
at the version it names. The files are in two open standards from Bitol, a Linux
Foundation project: ODCS for a contract and ODPS for the product. Nothing here needs a
platform or an account.
{%- endif %}

